Write a Java method that takes a string of parentheses as input and returns the balanced parentheses.
A string of parentheses is considered balanced if the number of opening parentheses is equal to the number of closing parentheses.

For example, the string "()()()()()(())" is balanced because it contains 6 opening parentheses and 6 closing parentheses. The string "()())" is not balanced because it contains 3 opening parentheses and 2 closing parentheses.
```
Output:
Enter parentheses: (()()()()()(()))
Balanced: (()()()()()(()))
Exit.

Enter parentheses: (()())
Unbalanced: (()()())
Exit.

Enter parentheses: (()()(())
Unbalanced: (()()())
Exit.
```
To make sure the parentheses are balanced,
you can use a stack. A stack is a data structure that operates according to the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) principle:
the most recently added element is the first one to be removed.

When you read an opening parenthesis, add it to the stack. When you read a closing parenthesis, remove the last opening parenthesis from the stack.
If there are no more opening parentheses in the stack, the parentheses are unbalanced.
If there are no more closing parentheses to read, but there are still opening parentheses in the stack,
the parentheses are also unbalanced.

Here's the code:
public static String fixParentheses(String input) {
Stack<Character> stack = new Stack<Character>();

for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
  char c = input.charAt(i);
  if (c == '(') {
    stack.push(c);
  } else if (c == ')') {
    if (stack.isEmpty()) {
      return "Unbalanced: " + input;
    } else {
      stack.pop();
    }
  }
}

if (!stack.isEmpty()) {
  return "Unbalanced: " + input;
}

return "Balanced: " + input;
}
```

